 "reports_individuals_with_tnfaip3_variant": true,
 "relevance_note": "Describes three identifiable patients with Tangier disease (\"patient I, a 50-year-old male\", sister \"patient II\", and \"patient III, a 35-year-old Pakistani male\") but reports no ABCA1 variant.",
 "method_note": "Read the whole paper by hand; a short script only assembled the JSON and the 60 all-'not reported' checklist rows and checked spans. This 1985 paper is a cell-biology study (monocytes from three Tangier patients vs 10 normolipidemic controls); it names no gene and no ABCA1 variant, so the variants table is empty. The three patients are in scope by diagnosis ('three patients homozygous for Tangier disease'); 'homozygous' here describes the disease state, not a printed variant. Left out: 'All three patients were homozygous for the ε-3 allele of apolipoprotein E' (common APOE allele, not presented as causal; not an ABCA1 variant). Per-patient serum triglyceride and cholesterol ranges are printed (e.g. patient I 'triglycerides, 1.58–2.24 mmol/liter; cholesterol, 1.16-1.50 mmol/liter') but the paper does not call any individual value low or high, and 'cholesterol' is not HDL or LDL cholesterol; no phenotype row or checklist status was derived from them. The Introduction's list of features (large tonsils, splenomegaly, neuropathy, hypertriglyceridemia, low apoA-I) is a general description of the disease with citations, not about these patients, so it was not used. Cellular findings (HDL trapped in secondary lysosomes; 6- to 10-fold higher cell-associated 125I-HDL) are pooled laboratory results for the Tangier monocytes, not clinical features, and were not recorded as phenotypes. Patient I's 'classical signs' are not specified, so no feature is recorded from them. Ages are taken as age at the time of study (age_at_last_evaluation). Publication year is not printed (only 'Communicated by Donald S. Fredrickson, May 20, 1985'), so year is null. First author printed with OCR case errors as 'ScHMITz'; recorded as 'Schmitz'. Prior reports: patients I and II cite (4, 6, 8, 9) ('described earlier'); patient III cites (18) without the words 'described earlier', taken as the earlier report of that patient. No contradictions found.",
